  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":19,"t":"single_choice","p":"A data set has mean 20, median 22 and mode 30. Which statement is most consistent with these values?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A data set has mean 20, median 22 and mode 30. Which statement is most consistent with these values?"}]}]},"e":"Mean < median < mode is the classic signature of a negatively (left) skewed distribution, where a few small values pull the mean down while the mode sits at the most frequent higher value.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"mean, mode and median","st":"mean, median and mode together","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":19,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Compare the order of mean, median and mode: a mean below the median suggests a low-value tail.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["The data are negatively skewed, with a tail of small values","The data are perfectly symmetrical","The data are positively skewed, with a tail of large values","The three measures cannot disagree"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":20,"t":"single_choice","p":"A survey asks students to choose one of five favourite sports. Which is the most suitable representation?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A survey asks students to choose one of five favourite sports. Which is the most suitable representation?"}]}]},"e":"The data are categorical with five named groups, so a bar chart (or a pie chart) compares the counts. A histogram and a cumulative frequency curve require numerical classes.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"classification of data","st":"choosing a representation","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":20,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Histograms, cumulative curves and box plots need numerical data. Named categories use bar or pie charts.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["A bar chart, because the data are categorical","A histogram, because there are five groups","A cumulative frequency curve, because the counts add to the total","A box plot, because it shows the spread of sports"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":21,"t":"single_choice","p":"A line graph of a child''s height is plotted monthly for two years and shows a steep rise. Why is a line graph appropriate here but misleading if the axes do not start at zero?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A line graph of a child''s height is plotted monthly for two years and shows a steep rise. Why is a line graph appropriate here but misleading if the axes do not start at zero?"}]}]},"e":"A line graph shows a continuous change over time, which suits monthly height. If the vertical axis starts at, say, 140 cm instead of 0, small changes look dramatic, which exaggerates the trend.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"line graphs","st":"misleading axes","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":21,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Check both axes before interpreting a line graph; a truncated vertical scale magnifies every fluctuation.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["It suits change over time, but a truncated vertical axis exaggerates small changes","It is inappropriate because height is continuous data","It is appropriate because height is a category","A pie chart would show the change better"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":22,"t":"single_choice","p":"A data set of 5 values has Σ(x − x̄)² = 80. What is the population standard deviation?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A data set of 5 values has Σ(x − x̄)² = 80. What is the population standard deviation?"}]}]},"e":"Variance = 80 ÷ 5 = 16, so σ = √16 = 4. Dividing by 5 (the number of values) is what distinguishes the population standard deviation from the sample version.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"standard deviation","st":"computing sigma from the sum of squares","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":22,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"σ = √(Σ(x − x̄)² / n). Divide by n first, then take the square root — not the other way round.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["4","16","8","80"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":23,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A hospital records, for each patient admitted in a week: age (years), blood group, number of previous admissions, and body temperature (°C).\n\n(a) Classify each of the four variables as qualitative or quantitative.\n(b) For the quantitative variables, state whether each is discrete or continuous.\n(c) Justify your classification of ''number of previous admissions''.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A hospital records, for each patient admitted in a week: age (years), blood group, number of previous admissions, and body temperature (°C)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Classify each of the four variables as qualitative or quantitative.\n(b) For the quantitative variables, state whether each is discrete or continuous.\n(c) Justify your classification of ''number of previous admissions''."}]}]},"e":"Blood group is qualitative; age and temperature are quantitative continuous; the number of previous admissions is quantitative discrete because it is a count with gaps between permitted values.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"classification of data","st":"classification of data","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":23,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"B(i)","next_time_hint":"Classify in two steps: is it a label or an amount, and if an amount, is it counted or measured?","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Name the qualitative variable.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["A qualitative variable is a label or category, not an amount.","Which of the four cannot sensibly be averaged?"],"strand":"B(i)","accepted":["blood group"]},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State which quantitative variable is discrete.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["A discrete variable is counted in whole steps.","Age and temperature can be measured more precisely; admissions cannot."],"strand":"B(ii)","accepted":["number of previous admissions"],"keywords":[["number"],["previous"],["admissions"]],"minKeywordGroups":3},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Justify why ''number of previous admissions'' must be discrete.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Can a patient have 2.4 previous admissions?","The values jump from one whole number to the next."],"strand":"B(iii)","accepted":["you can only count whole admissions, so its values are separate whole numbers"],"keywords":[["count"],["whole"],["admissions"],["values"],["separate"],["numbers"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for blood group. (b) 2 marks: 1 for admissions discrete, 1 for age/temperature continuous. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''counted'', 1 for the consequence (no fractional values).","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for blood group. (b) 2 marks: 1 for admissions discrete, 1 for age/temperature continuous. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''counted'', 1 for the consequence (no fractional values).","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"In (c) explain the counting argument, not only the definition of discrete."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for blood group. (b) 2 marks: 1 for admissions discrete, 1 for age/temperature continuous. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''counted'', 1 for the consequence (no fractional values)."},{"n":24,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The masses of 40 parcels, in kilograms, are summarised below.\n\n10 ≤ m < 15: 6 parcels\n15 ≤ m < 20: 14 parcels\n20 ≤ m < 30: 12 parcels\n30 ≤ m < 40: 8 parcels\n\n(a) State the class width of the third class.\n(b) Calculate the estimated mean mass, using midpoints.\n(c) Explain why the class 20 ≤ m < 30 has a lower frequency density than 15 ≤ m < 20 even though it has almost the same frequency.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The masses of 40 parcels, in kilograms, are summarised below."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"10 ≤ m < 15: 6 parcels\n15 ≤ m < 20: 14 parcels\n20 ≤ m < 30: 12 parcels\n30 ≤ m < 40: 8 parcels"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the class width of the third class.\n(b) Calculate the estimated mean mass, using midpoints.\n(c) Explain why the class 20 ≤ m < 30 has a lower frequency density than 15 ≤ m < 20 even though it has almost the same frequency."}]}]},"e":"Σf x = 6(12.5) + 14(17.5) + 12(25) + 8(35) = 75 + 245 + 300 + 280 = 900, so the estimated mean is 900/40 = 22.5 kg. Densities are 1.2, 2.8, 1.2 and 0.8; the third class is less dense than the second because its frequency is spread over twice the width.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"grouped data","st":"grouped data","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":24,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Estimated mean = Σ(f × midpoint) ÷ Σf. Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the class width of 20 ≤ m < 30.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Width = upper boundary − lower boundary.","30 − 20."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":10,"tolerance":0,"unit":"kg"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate Σ(f × midpoint).","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Use the midpoint of each class: 12.5, 17.5, 25, 35.","Multiply each midpoint by its frequency and add the four products."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":900,"tolerance":0,"unit":"kg"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the estimated mean mass, in kilograms.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Divide your Σ(f × midpoint) by the total frequency.","The frequencies add to 40."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":22.5,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"kg"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain the lower frequency density of the third class.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width.","Compare 12/10 with 14/5."],"strand":"C(iv)","accepted":["its class width is twice as large, so the same frequency is spread over a wider interval, giving a lower density: 1.2 against 2.8"],"keywords":[["class"],["width"],["twice"],["large"],["frequency"],["spread"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 10. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the four products, 1 for 900. (c) 2 marks: 1 for dividing by 40, 1 for 22.5. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the density formula, 1 for the comparison 1.2 vs 2.8.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 10. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the four products, 1 for 900. (c) 2 marks: 1 for dividing by 40, 1 for 22.5. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the density formula, 1 for the comparison 1.2 vs 2.8.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","statistics"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Build the grouped table in the statistics workspace as a check, but record your answers in the boxes.","statistics_chart":"histogram","response_stats":{"title":"Your statistics construction","instructions":"Build the grouped table in the statistics workspace as a check, but record your answers in the boxes.","chart":"histogram","starter":{"chart":"histogram"}}},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 10. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the four products, 1 for 900. (c) 2 marks: 1 for dividing by 40, 1 for 22.5. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the density formula, 1 for the comparison 1.2 vs 2.8."},{"n":25,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The waiting times, in minutes, of 15 patients are:\n\n4, 6, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12, 13, 15, 16, 18, 20, 23, 25, 28\n\n(a) State the median.\n(b) Calculate the lower and upper quartiles.\n(c) Calculate the interquartile range.\n(d) A waiting time of 45 minutes is recorded the next day. Explain, using the 1.5 × IQR rule, whether it is an outlier.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The waiting times, in minutes, of 15 patients are:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4, 6, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12, 13, 15, 16, 18, 20, 23, 25, 28"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the median.\n(b) Calculate the lower and upper quartiles.\n(c) Calculate the interquartile range.\n(d) A waiting time of 45 minutes is recorded the next day. Explain, using the 1.5 × IQR rule, whether it is an outlier."}]}]},"e":"Median 13; Q1 = 8; Q3 = 20; IQR = 12. The upper fence is 20 + 1.5(12) = 38, so 45 minutes lies beyond it and is an outlier. The box plot''s long upper whisker anticipates this.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"five-number summary and box plots","st":"five-number summary and box plots","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":25,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Q1 and Q3 are the medians of the lower and upper halves. The 1.5 × IQR fences identify outliers.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the median waiting time, in minutes.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["With 15 values, which position is the middle?","(15 + 1) ÷ 2 = 8th value."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":13,"tolerance":0,"unit":"minutes"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State the lower quartile Q1, in minutes.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Exclude the median and take the lower seven values.","The middle of 4, 6, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12 is the 4th value."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":8,"tolerance":0,"unit":"minutes"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State the upper quartile Q3, in minutes.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The upper half is 15, 16, 18, 20, 23, 25, 28.","Its middle value is the 4th."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":20,"tolerance":0,"unit":"minutes"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Calculate the IQR, in minutes.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The interquartile range is the upper quartile minus the lower quartile.","Subtract your Q1 from your Q3."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":12,"tolerance":0,"unit":"minutes"},{"id":"e","label":"(e)","prompt":"Decide whether 45 minutes is an outlier and justify with the fence value.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Upper fence = Q3 + 1.5 × IQR.","Compare 45 with 20 + 18."],"strand":"C(iv)","accepted":["yes, it is an outlier: the upper fence is 38 minutes and 45 exceeds it"],"keywords":[["outlier"],["upper"],["fence"],["minutes"],["exceeds"]],"minKeywordGroups":3}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for splitting at the median, 1 for 8. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the upper half, 1 for 20. (d) 1 mark for 12. (e) 2 marks: 1 for the fence 38, 1 for the correct conclusion.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for splitting at the median, 1 for 8. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the upper half, 1 for 20. (d) 1 mark for 12. (e) 2 marks: 1 for the fence 38, 1 for the correct conclusion.","response_tools":["statistics","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"statistics","response_guidance":"Enter the 15 waiting times in the statistics workspace and draw the box plot to check your quartiles.","statistics_chart":"box_plot","response_stats":{"title":"Your statistics construction","instructions":"Enter the 15 waiting times in the statistics workspace and draw the box plot to check your quartiles.","chart":"box_plot","starter":{"chart":"box_plot"}}},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for splitting at the median, 1 for 8. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the upper half, 1 for 20. (d) 1 mark for 12. (e) 2 marks: 1 for the fence 38, 1 for the correct conclusion."},{"n":26,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A grouped frequency table for 50 journey times (minutes) is:\n\n0 ≤ t < 5: 4\n5 ≤ t < 10: 12\n10 ≤ t < 20: 18\n20 ≤ t < 30: 10\n30 ≤ t < 40: 6\n\n(a) Calculate the class width of each class.\n(b) Calculate the frequency density of the class 10 ≤ t < 20.\n(c) Explain why plain frequencies cannot be used as bar heights for this table.\n(d) Calculate the total area of the correct histogram and state what it represents.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A grouped frequency table for 50 journey times (minutes) is:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"0 ≤ t < 5: 4\n5 ≤ t < 10: 12\n10 ≤ t < 20: 18\n20 ≤ t < 30: 10\n30 ≤ t < 40: 6"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the class width of each class.\n(b) Calculate the frequency density of the class 10 ≤ t < 20.\n(c) Explain why plain frequencies cannot be used as bar heights for this table.\n(d) Calculate the total area of the correct histogram and state what it represents."}]}]},"e":"The densities 0.8, 2.4, 1.8, 1.0, 0.6 give areas 4, 12, 18, 10, 6 which total 50 — exactly the number of journeys. Area is frequency; height alone is not.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"histograms","st":"histograms","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":26,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Draw a histogram only from frequency densities when class widths differ: area must equal frequency.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the class width of 20 ≤ t < 30.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["A class width is the upper boundary minus the lower boundary.","For 20 ≤ t < 30 use 30 − 20."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":10,"tolerance":0,"unit":"minutes"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the frequency density of 10 ≤ t < 20.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width.","18 ÷ 10."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":1.8,"tolerance":0.01},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain why frequencies cannot be used as bar heights here.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare the widths: 5 and 10 minutes.","A bar''s area must be proportional to the frequency."],"strand":"C(iii)","accepted":["the class widths are not equal, so equal frequencies would give different areas; heights must be density so that area represents frequency"],"keywords":[["class"],["widths"],["equal"],["frequencies"],["give"],["different"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Calculate the total area of the histogram.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Add density × width for all five classes.","It must equal the total frequency."],"strand":"C(iv)","answer":50,"tolerance":0}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 1.8. (c) 2 marks: 1 for unequal widths, 1 for the area argument. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the summation, 1 for 50 = total frequency.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 1.8. (c) 2 marks: 1 for unequal widths, 1 for the area argument. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the summation, 1 for 50 = total frequency.","response_tools":["statistics","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"statistics","response_guidance":"Enter the grouped table in the statistics workspace: the chart will plot densities automatically when widths differ.","statistics_chart":"histogram","response_stats":{"title":"Your statistics construction","instructions":"Enter the grouped table in the statistics workspace: the chart will plot densities automatically when widths differ.","chart":"histogram","starter":{"chart":"histogram"}}},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 1.8. (c) 2 marks: 1 for unequal widths, 1 for the area argument. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the summation, 1 for 50 = total frequency."}]'::jsonb) as x
